  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM MDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nWV imagery continues to see the upper level low to the northeast\nnow into Canada with positively tilted troughing stretching to \nthe southeast across the northern Rockies. IR shows clears skies\nwith nothing of note on radar for the CWA. Relatively light \nwinds into Tuesday with areas of smoke continuing through much \nof the day until the aforementioned low pushes further east and\nhigh pressure builds back in from the south. On the backside of\nthis upper level low, a few sporadic showers possible (10-15%) \nfor the northern Absarokas and Bighorns, aided by terrain \ninduced development for the late afternoon and early evening \nhours. No impacts expected with little to no accumulation \nbefore quickly pushing east and dying off before sunset and into\nthe overnight hours Tuesday. Temperatures similar to Monday \nafternoon remaining on the cooler side relative to the weekend \nand last week. \n\nBeyond Tuesday, expect dry conditions a warming trend through\nthe remainder of the work week and into the weekend. Northwest\nflow continues across the state as the main high to the south\nbuilds in with increasing convergence aloft. As the upper level\nflow weakens by Thursday and Friday, temperatures push back \nabove average 15 to 20 degrees. Widespread 80s west of the \nDivide and 90s to possible triple digits once again for portions\neast of the Divide. Heat and dry conditions extend into the \nweekend and early next week. Fire weather conditions will only \nremain elevated during this time with no stronger winds expected\nas the low level flow weakens as well with a degraded pressure \ngradient across much of the state. Smoke conditions will \nimprove Wednesday onward as well, with the high pressure \nbuilding in from the south and aforementioned low well to the \neast by this point. \n\nUpper level ridging continues but becomes more zonal by early\nnext week as a trough extends across the Pacific northwest. \nThis will give some possible better chances of precipitation \nnext week with not so hot temperatures as well, but still above \naverage with no significant weather pattern change on the \nhorizon otherwise.",
